# Task Q: compiled-code fibers (runner/tlk_fiber.c).
#
# When compiled code reaches the frame deadline or a pending IRQ, the bridge
# used to unwind the whole host call chain to the scheduler frame and resume
# the guest by INTERPRETING from the yield PC: the rest of every routine on
# that chain (the SPC upload loop $C22029, the main loop, ...) then ran on the
# interpreter, ~half of all interpreted opcodes. The scheduler run now runs on
# a separate C stack (a fiber). At those yield points the fiber switches back
# to the frame driver with the resume PC published exactly as before; the
# driver delivers the interrupt / ends the field and calls
# run_until_quiescent(PC) again, which switches back into the suspended chain
# when PC, S, PB and the widths still match (otherwise the chain is unwound
# the old way). The bridge's per-run state below belongs to whichever stack is
# running and is swapped at every switch.

